**Title:** Planetary-Cell Mobile Handset with State-Address Core, Live Orbital Link Management, and On-Device Post-Quantum Identity (BUPCP-001)

**Inventor:** C. Matthew Blackwell — Independent Designer

**Design records:** EQS-1281 (design), EQS-1282 (live-sky run), EQS-1283 (software build and first call), EQS-1284 (publication package) — all dated 17 November 2026, payload-hashed, identity-baked.

## FIELD OF THE INVENTION

The invention relates to mobile communication handsets, and in particular to a handset whose primary serving cell is an orbital layer rather than a terrestrial tower grid, which determines its own address as a state rather than by carrier account, which manages its satellite link live from propagated orbital elements, and which holds its communication identity as a post-quantum keypair generated and retained on the device.

## BACKGROUND

Conventional cellular handsets depend on (i) a terrestrial tower grid, so that coverage ends where the grid ends; (ii) a carrier account and, in current practice, a vendor cloud account, so that a failure of a central sign-in or account service disables functions of a handset the user physically holds — a failure mode observed at national scale on 17 November 2026, when a major vendor's sign-in, store, cloud, and care services were simultaneously disrupted. Satellite capability in current handsets is an emergency or messaging fallback, or a carrier-sold add-on layered on the account model. No current handset determines its serving satellite, link state, and handover schedule itself from live orbital elements; none addresses itself by state; and none holds its identity solely on-device in post-quantum form. The present invention addresses each of these.

## SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION

A handset (BUPCP-001) comprises: (a) an orbital radio subsystem operating in direct-to-cell satellite bands as the primary cell, with terrestrial cellular as fallback; (b) a sky engine that ingests two-line orbital element sets, propagates them (SGP4), and for the handset's position and any time returns the set of satellites above an elevation mask and a serving satellite selected by link quality; (c) a link manager that applies a link budget live to the serving satellite, computes signal-to-noise ratio in at least two channel widths, and selects the channel; (d) a state core that computes the handset's address as a state — its host body's register value under a filed packet form together with its coordinates — independent of any account registry; (e) an identity module holding a post-quantum keypair (ML-KEM-1024) generated on the device, by which messages are encapsulated handset-to-handset (HKDF-SHA256 key derivation, AES-256-GCM); and (f) a transmission gate that releases a message only when the live link closes at both the sending and receiving endpoints in the same time step. The serving satellite changes on the order of once per minute in low Earth orbit; the handset maintains the cell by continuous handover driven by the sky engine's schedule.

