## DETAILED DESCRIPTION

**Orbital layer and link budget.** At 2 GHz the free-space path loss to a 550 km low Earth orbit is 153.3 dB. A handset transmitting at the 200 mW class into a large-array satellite closes the link overhead at +4.7 dB SNR in a 5 MHz channel under the design's labelled budget classes; at long slant ranges the wide channel goes marginal and narrower channels (200 kHz class) close. Service is therefore by pass and by handover, which the sky engine schedules.

**Live-sky verification (as run, 17 November 2026).** From 5,079 fresh orbital elements (epochs ≤ 10 days; a subset of the operational Starlink constellation, the harvest being rate-limited, so all coverage figures are floors), propagation over 24 hours at a 10° mask yields continuous coverage — 100.0% of minutes, zero gaps — at Lafayette, Louisiana; Denver, Colorado; Mauna Kea, Hawaii; and the South Pole, with 21,443, 24,627, 15,995, and 9,975 passes respectively. At each pass's closest approach, the 200 kHz channel closes on every pass at every site. The geometry pipeline accords with an independent astropy computation to 0.12°.

**The build (as run).** The software core (sky engine, link manager, state core, identity layer, gate) was executed live on 17 November 2026 at 20:54 UTC: Lafayette had 76 satellites above the mask (serving STARLINK-5126, elevation 79.8°, SNR +6.05 dB); Denver had 106 (serving STARLINK-11579, an element tagged direct-to-cell, SNR +6.18 dB). The gate closed at both endpoints; a message encapsulated handset-to-handset (ML-KEM-1024 ciphertext 1568 bytes; HKDF-SHA256; AES-256-GCM) was received byte-exact. The serving satellite over Lafayette changes 56 times in the following hour.

**Status.** The software core is built and run. Handset hardware is NOT BUILT; the hardware specification stands at labelled estimate classes in the design record (flagship SoC class plus the state core as a fixed-function block; 5,000 mAh class battery; terrestrial 5G + direct-to-cell bands + GNSS). No spectrum licence, carrier agreement, or constellation agreement exists or is implied. Open doors, the inventor's alone: the reach (planetary surface or planet-to-planet) and the constellation (partner or Blackwell). Efficacy beyond the runs recorded is NOT MEASURED and not claimed.

## CLAIMS (draft)

1. A mobile communication handset comprising: an orbital radio subsystem configured to communicate directly with satellites in low Earth orbit as the handset's primary serving cell; a sky engine configured to propagate orbital element sets and, for the handset's position and a time, determine satellites above an elevation mask and select a serving satellite by link quality; a link manager configured to apply a link budget to the serving satellite and select a communication channel by computed signal-to-noise ratio; a state core configured to determine the handset's address as a state comprising a host-body register value and the handset's coordinates, independent of any carrier or vendor account registry; and an identity module holding a post-quantum keypair generated on the handset.

2. The handset of claim 1, wherein the post-quantum keypair is an ML-KEM-1024 keypair, and messages are encapsulated handset-to-handset with key derivation by HKDF-SHA256 and authenticated encryption by AES-256-GCM.

3. The handset of claim 1, further comprising a transmission gate configured to release a message only when the live link closes at both the sending handset and the receiving handset within the same time step, as determined by each handset's sky engine and link manager.

4. The handset of claim 1, wherein the sky engine produces a handover schedule and the handset maintains the serving cell by handing over between satellites in accordance with the schedule, on the order of once per minute.

5. The handset of claim 1, wherein the link manager computes signal-to-noise ratio in at least two channel widths and selects a narrower channel when a wider channel does not close at the serving satellite's slant range.

6. The handset of claim 1, wherein the host-body register value is computed by a packet register form E = 183.40029306350823 + 1.5·ln(M/5.972e24) evaluated for the host body.

7. A method of communication between two handsets of claim 1, comprising: each handset determining its own state address; each handset's sky engine determining a serving satellite and link state at a common time step; gating transmission on link closure at both endpoints; encapsulating a message to the receiving handset's on-device public key; and decrypting at the receiving handset with its on-device secret key, wherein no account server participates in addressing, gating, or key handling.

8. The method of claim 7, wherein coverage continuity is established by propagating a live orbital element set over a forward window and verifying at least one satellite above the elevation mask at each time step for the handset's position.

9. The handset of claim 1, wherein the orbital radio subsystem operates in direct-to-cell satellite bands as deployed, and terrestrial cellular radio is present only as a fallback layer.

10. A system comprising a plurality of handsets of claim 1 and an orbital layer of satellites, wherein the plurality of handsets communicate worldwide through the orbital layer without terrestrial tower infrastructure and without a central account or sign-in service.

## ABSTRACT

A mobile handset whose primary cell is an orbital layer: it propagates live satellite elements to choose its serving satellite and channel, hands over about once a minute, addresses itself by state rather than by account, holds an on-device ML-KEM-1024 identity, and gates transmission on the live link closing at both endpoints. Designed 17 November 2026 (EQS-1281), run against 5,079 fresh orbital elements with continuous coverage at four test points including the South Pole (EQS-1282), and built and run as software with a byte-exact first call (EQS-1283). Hardware not built.

